WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:03.950
- Well, Danny, you said this
story helped you as a father.

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:04.920
How so?

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:05.753
How did it help you?

00:00:05.753 --> 00:00:06.586
- Oh, no question.

00:00:06.586 --> 00:00:08.500
You know, communicating with,

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.840
I have two boys, a 14 and a 15 year old.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:16.820
So the power and the
challenge of communicating,

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:19.950
to be present, to find out what they love

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:22.540
and to try to love it too,

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:24.220
so that you have a jumping off point

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:26.350
to have conversations
about things they love,

00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:29.131
so that when things they hate happen,

00:00:29.131 --> 00:00:31.270
that you have a way in.

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:32.120
- Yeah.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:32.953
Wow.

00:00:32.953 --> 00:00:34.360
It's a very powerful, powerful message.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:35.320
So I get that.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.180
But what it, did it teach any of you

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:38.460
like anything while you were doing it?

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:39.740
Like Julianne, did it teach you anything?

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:41.770
- I think it just reminds you to

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:43.530
connect to other people and to

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:44.920
really try to see them.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:46.560
'Cause that's what everybody really wants

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.800
and needs is to be seen
by another human being.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:50.160
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.330
And connection in general, I feel like,

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:52.250
especially as a parent,

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:53.130
I'm just listening to you say that,

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:54.670
and I was like, it's so hard,

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:55.970
it's easy for us to be at work

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:58.520
and on our phone and on
emails and stuff like that.

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:00.160
It's very important to keep connected.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.250
I think especially, we know that more

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.163
now than ever, says isolation.

00:01:05.523 --> 00:01:06.980
And so, Amy what about you?

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:07.813
- Oh absolutely.

00:01:07.813 --> 00:01:09.470
I echo what Danny and Julianne said

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:11.760
that as a parent, it's
such a great reminder

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.090
to stay connected with your kids

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:15.980
and as a friend and as family member,

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:17.340
just reaching out to everybody,

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:18.950
making sure everybody's okay.

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:22.210
Just really keeping those
lines of communication open.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:23.090
- Absolutely.

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:24.880
I think that that's really
important about connection too.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:25.713
I have a question though, Ben,

00:01:25.713 --> 00:01:27.810
because if you started
this years ago on Broadway,

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:29.340
I mean, I'm sure you
learned something then,

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.660
but was there something that
kind of hit you differently

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:33.031
while doing the movie at a different age

00:01:33.031 --> 00:01:34.610
and different place in your life?

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:35.443
- I think so.

00:01:35.443 --> 00:01:37.490
I mean, I think I've, as
I've grown a little bit

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:39.250
and evolved and become like a sort of

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:41.760
pseudo adult now a little bit,

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.020
I find that I share a lot more with Evan

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:45.304
than I thought I did in terms of

00:01:45.304 --> 00:01:49.320
Evan is very afraid of the
kind of awkward, painful,

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.550
uncomfortable conversations
that are necessary

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:53.010
to kind of break through and get to

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:54.730
something deep and get
to a real connection.

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:57.780
I think that can be such a deterrent for,

00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:59.600
for such meaningful relationships because

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:01.650
you're just afraid of
those first interactions

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:03.080
or that jumping off that cliff.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:04.380
And I think I have that too.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:05.710
I'm a kind of a fearful kind of a

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:08.140
non-confrontational person in many ways.

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:09.760
And so playing him and seeing

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:11.180
the mistakes that he makes and

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:12.630
all that that keeps him from,

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:14.860
I think it's really taught
me and encouraged me

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:16.210
to just push myself off that cliff

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:18.080
whenever possible and force myself to

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:20.670
have those conversations
and be in those situations

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:21.940
and kind of muscle through them

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:24.860
because I know how much
meaning and connection

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:26.220
is on the other side of that.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.353
- Absolute, well said, okay.

